"CSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn1189view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/\",\"lineno\":1180}); Total lines: 2053\nL1072: cite130†<semicolon-token> L1073: cite153†<EOF-token> L1086: Note: If the result contains cite259†lists†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite242†declarations , how they’re materialized in the CSSOM depends on the rule. Some turn them all into cite310†nested declarations rules , others will treat them all as declarations, and others will treat the first item differently from the rest.\nL1087: \nL1088: If `rule` is valid in the current context, return it. Otherwise, return nothing.\nL1089: \nL1090: anything else\nL1091:     cite304†Consume a component value from `input` and append the returned value to `rule`’s prelude.\nL1092: #### 5.5.3. Consume a qualified rule\nL1093: \nL1094: To consume a qualified rule, from a cite237†token stream `input`, given an optional cite257†token `stop token` and an optional bool `nested` (default false):\nL1095: \nL1096: Let `rule` be a new cite106†qualified rule with its prelude, declarations, and child rules all initially set to empty cite259†lists†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1097: \nL1098: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1099: cite153†<EOF-token> L1100: `stop token` (if passed)\nL1101:     This is a cite207†parse error . Return nothing.\nL1102: cite132†<}-token> L1103:     This is a cite207†parse error . If `nested` is true, return nothing. Otherwise, cite308†consume a token and append the result to `rule`’s prelude.\nL1104: cite131†<{-token> L1105:     If the first two non-cite166†<whitespace-token> values of `rule`’s prelude are an cite154†<ident-token> whose value starts with \"--\" followed by a cite202†<colon-token> , then:\nL1106:   * If `nested` is true, cite311†consume the remnants of a bad declaration from `input`, with `nested` set to true, and return nothing.\nL1107: \nL1108:   * If `nested` is false, cite309†consume a block from `input`, and return nothing.\nL1109: \nL1110: What’s this check for?\nL1111: cite242†Declarations and cite106†qualified rules don’t generally overlap in their allowed syntax. No currently-defined CSS property allows {}-blocks in its value, so `foo:bar {};` is definitely a rule, and `foo: bar;` is definitely a property.\nL1112: Even if a future CSS property allows {}-blocks in its value, the allowed syntax is restricted to the {}-block being the whole value, such as `foo: {...};`, which is guaranteed to not be a valid rule, since the : doesn’t have an ident or function following it to mark it as a pseudo-class.\nL1113: This allows us to mix declarations and rules in the same context: we first try to parse something as a declaration, and if that doesn’t result in a valid declaration, we reparse it as a rule instead. An accidentally-invalid declaration will parse as a rule instead, but that’s fine: the parser will stop at the declaration’s ending semicolon and consider it an invalid rule.\nL1114: (Or in the case of a property containing a {}-block, will stop just *before* the semicolon, still considering it an invalid rule, and then the next attempt to parse something will throw out the lone semicolon as invalid.) So the total amount of tokens consumed is the same regardless.\nL1115: cite312†Custom properties , however, don’t have the CSSWG carefully vetting their syntax. Authors can write a custom property that takes a {}-block in its value, even combined with other things; if that custom property is then invalid (due to an invalidly-written cite313†var() function, for example), when it’s reparsed as a rule it will stop early, at the {}-block.\nL1116: The remaining tokens of the custom property’s value will then get parsed as a fresh construct, potentially causing unexpected declarations or rules to be created.\nL1117: To avoid this (admittedly very niche) corner-case, we subtract the syntax of a cite312†custom property from that of a cite106†qualified rule ; if you’re in a context that allows properties and rules to be mixed, and you somehow end up parsing a rule that looks like a custom property, you’ve messed up, and need to instead consume an entire custom property (all the way to the ending semicolon).\nL1118: (If we’re in a context that doesn’t allow properties, we just throw away the rule if it looks like a custom property. This ensures that `--foo:hover { color: blue; }` is consistently invalid everywhere, without potentially consuming a ton of a stylesheet looking for the non-existent ending semicolon.)\nL1119: Otherwise, cite309†consume a block from `input`, and let `child rules` be the result. If the first item of `child rules` is a cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite242†declarations , remove it from `child rules` and assign it to `rule`’s declarations. If any remaining items of `child rules` are lists of declarations, replace them with cite310†nested declarations rules containing the list as its sole child. Assign `child rules` to `rule`’s child cite238†rules .\nL1120: If `rule` is valid in the current context, return it; otherwise return an invalid rule error.\nL1121: \nL1122: Tests\nL1123: \nL1124:   * cite314†invalid-nested-rules.html†wpt.fyi cite315†(live test)†wpt.live cite316†(source)†github.com L1125: anything else\nL1126:     cite304†Consume a component value from `input` and append the result to `rule`’s prelude.\nL1127: Tests\nL1128:   * cite317†custom-property-rule-ambiguity.html†wpt.fyi cite318†(live test)†wpt.live cite319†(source)†github.com L1129:   * cite314†invalid-nested-rules.html†wpt.fyi cite315†(live test)†wpt.live cite316†(source)†github.com L1130: #### 5.5.4. Consume a block\nL1131: \nL1132: To consume a block, from a cite237†token stream `input`:\nL1133: \nL1134: Assert: The cite265†next token is a cite131†<{-token> .\nL1135: \nL1136: cite268†Discard a token from `input`. cite320†Consume a block’s contents from `input` and let `rules` be the result. Discard a token from `input`.\nL1137: \nL1138: Return `rules`.\nL1139: #### 5.5.5. Consume a block’s contents\nL1140: \nL1141: To consume a block’s contents from a cite237†token stream `input`:\nL1142: \nL1143: Note: This algorithm returns a cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org , consisting of cite238†rules and lists of cite242†declarations . Depending on the parent context, a list of declarations might be materialized in the CSSOM as either a `cite321†CSSStyleDeclaration `, or as a `cite322†CSSNestedDeclarations ` rule.\nL1144: Let `rules` be an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org , containing either cite238†rules or lists of cite242†declarations .\nL1145: \nL1146: Let `decls` be an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite242†declarations .\nL1147: \nL1148: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1149: cite166†<whitespace-token> L1150: cite130†<semicolon-token> L1151:     cite268†Discard a token from `input`.\nL1152: cite153†<EOF-token> L1153: cite132†<}-token> L1154:     Return `rules`.\nL1155: cite129†<at-keyword-token> L1156:     If `decls` is not empty, append it to `rules`, and set `decls` to a fresh empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite242†declarations .\nL1157: \nL1158: cite300†Consume an at-rule from `input`, with `nested` set to true. If a cite238†rule was returned, append it to `rules`.\nL1159: \nL1160: anything else\nL1161:     cite260†Mark `input`.\nL1162: cite263†Consume a declaration from `input`, with `nested` set to true. If a cite242†declaration was returned, append it to `decls`, and cite323†discard a mark from `input`.\nL1163: \nL1164: Otherwise, cite324†restore a mark from `input`, then cite302†consume a qualified rule from `input`, with `nested` set to true, and cite130†<semicolon-token> as the `stop token`.\nL1165: \nL1166: If nothing was returned\nL1167: \nL1168: \nL1169: Do nothing\nL1170: \nL1171: If an cite303†invalid rule error was returned\nL1172: If `decls` is not empty, append `decls` to `rules`, and set `decls` to a fresh empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite242†declarations . (Otherwise, do nothing.)\nL1173: \nL1174: If a cite238†rule was returned\nL1175: \nL1176: \nL1177: If `decls` is not empty, append `decls` to `rules`, and set `decls` to a fresh empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite242†declarations . Append the cite238†rule to `rules`.\nL1178: \nL1179: Implementation note\nL1180: This spec, as with many CSS specs, has been written to prioritize understandability over efficiency. A number of algorithms, notably the above \"parse as a declaration, then parse as a rule\" behavior can be fairly inefficient if implemented naively as described.\nL1181: \nL1182: However, the behavior has been carefully written to allow \"early exits\" as much as possible. In particular, and roughly in order of when the exit can occur:\nL1183:   * If the first non-whitespace token isn’t an cite154†<ident-token> for a recognized property name (or a custom property name), you can immediately stop parsing as a declaration and reparse as a rule instead. If the next non-whitespace token isn’t a cite202†<colon-token> , you can similarly immediately stop parsing as a declaration.\nL1184: \nL1185: (That is, font+ ... is guaranteed to not be a property, nor is not-a-prop-name: ....)\nL1186:   * If the first two non-whitespace tokens are a custom property name and a colon, it’s definitely a custom property and won’t ever produce a valid rule, so even if the custom property ends up invalid there’s no need to try and reparse as a rule.\nL1187: \nL1188: (That is, --foo:hover {...} is guaranteed to be a custom property, not a rule.)\nL1189:   * If the first three non-whitespace tokens are a valid property name, a colon, and anything other than a cite131†<{-token> , and then while parsing the declaration’s value you encounter a <{-token>, you can immediately stop parsing as a declaration and reparse as a rule instead.\nL1190: \nL1191: (That is, font:bar {... is guaranteed to be an invalid property.)\nL1192:   * If you see a recognized property name, a colon, and a {}-block, but the first non-whitespace tokens following that block isn’t either immediately the final semicolon, or the !important followed by the semicolon, you can immediately stop parsing as a declaration and reparse as a rule instead.\nL1193: \nL1194: (That is, cite325†font: {} bar ... is guaranteed to be an invalid property; you don’t need to keep parsing until you hit a semicolon.)\nL1195: Similarly, even tho the parsing requirements are specified to rely on checking the grammar of the declarations as you parse, a generic processor trying to implement a non-CSS language on top of the generic CSS syntax can still get away with just verifying that declarations start with an ident, a colon, and then either contain solely a {}-block or no {}-block at all. They’ll just spent a little more time on parsing than an implementation with grammar knowledge in cases like foo:hover ...\nL1196: {}, since they can’t early-exit on the first token.\nL1197: Tests\nL1198: \nL1199:   * cite326†at-rule-in-declaration-list.html†wpt.fyi cite327†(live test)†wpt.live cite328†(source)†github.com L1200: #### 5.5.6. Consume a declaration\nL1201: \nL1202: To consume a declaration from a cite237†token stream `input`, given an optional bool `nested` (default false):\nL1203: \nL1204: Let `decl` be a new cite242†declaration , with an initially empty name and a value set to an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1205: \nL1206:   1. If the cite265†next token is an cite154†<ident-token> , cite308†consume a token from `input` and set `decl`'s name to the token’s value.\nL1207: Otherwise, cite311†consume the remnants of a bad declaration from `input`, with `nested`, and return nothing.\nL1208: \nL1209:   2. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL1210:   3. If the cite265†next token is a cite202†<colon-token> , cite268†discard a token from `input`.\nL1211: \nL1212: Otherwise, cite311†consume the remnants of a bad declaration from `input`, with `nested`, and return nothing.\nL1213:   4. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL1214:   5. cite305†Consume a list of component values from `input`, with `nested`, and with cite130†<semicolon-token> as the stop token, and set `decl`’s value to the result.\nL1215:   6. If the last two non-cite166†<whitespace-token> s in `decl`’s value are a cite159†<delim-token> with the value \"!\" followed by an cite154†<ident-token> with a value that is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"important\", remove them from `decl`’s value and set `decl`’s `important` flag.\nL1216:   7. While the last item in `decl`’s value is a cite166†<whitespace-token> , cite329†remove†infra.spec.whatwg.org that token.\nL1217:   8. If `decl`’s name is a cite330†custom property name string†drafts.css-houdini.org , then set `decl`’s `original text` to the segment of the original source text string corresponding to the tokens of `decl`’s value.\nL1218: Otherwise, if `decl`’s value contains a top-level cite249†simple block with an associated token of cite131†<{-token> , and also contains any other non-cite166†<whitespace-token> value, return nothing. (That is, a top-level {}-block is only allowed as the entire value of a non-custom property.)\nL1219: Otherwise, if `decl`’s name is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"unicode-range\", cite235†consume the value of a unicode-range descriptor from the segment of the original source text string corresponding to the tokens returned by the cite305†consume a list of component values call, and replace `decl`’s value with the result.\nL1220: Tests\nL1221:      * cite331†var-with-blocks.html†wpt.fyi cite332†(live test)†wpt.live cite333†(source)†github.com L1222:   9. If `decl` is valid in the current context, return it; otherwise return nothing.\nL1223: \nL1224: To consume the remnants of a bad declaration from a cite237†token stream `input`, given a bool `nested`:\nL1225: \nL1226: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1227: \nL1228: cite153†<eof-token> L1229: cite130†<semicolon-token> L1230: \nL1231: \nL1232: cite268†Discard a token from `input`, and return nothing.\nL1233: \nL1234: cite132†<}-token> L1235: If `nested` is true, return nothing. Otherwise, cite268†discard a token .\nL1236: \nL1237: anything else\nL1238: \nL1239: \nL1240: cite304†Consume a component value from `input`, and do nothing.\nL1241: #### 5.5.7. Consume a list of component values\nL1242: \nL1243: To consume a list of component values from a cite237†token stream `input`, given an optional cite257†token `stop token` and an optional boolean `nested` (default false):\nL1244: \nL1245: Let `values` be an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org of cite240†component values .\nL1246: \nL1247: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1248: \nL1249: cite153†<eof-token> L1250: `stop token` (if passed)\nL1251: \nL1252: \nL1253: Return `values`.\nL1254: \nL1255: cite132†<}-token> L1256: \nL1257: \nL1258: If `nested` is true, return `values`.\nL1259: Otherwise, this is a cite207†parse error . cite308†Consume a token from `input` and append the result to `values`.\nL1260: \nL1261: anything else\nL1262: \nL1263: \nL1264: cite304†Consume a component value from `input`, and append the result to `values`.\nL1265: \nL1266: Tests\nL1267: \nL1268:   * cite334†declarations-trim-whitespace.html†wpt.fyi cite335†(live test)†wpt.live cite336†(source)†github.com L1269: #### 5.5.8. Consume a component value\nL1270: \nL1271: To consume a component value from a cite237†token stream `input`:\nL1272: \nL1273: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1274: \nL1275: cite131†<{-token> L1276: cite203†<[-token> L1277: cite195†<(-token> L1278: \nL1279: \nL1280: cite337†Consume a simple block from `input` and return the result.\nL1281: \nL1282: cite155†<function-token> L1283: \nL1284: \nL1285: cite338†Consume a function from `input` and return the result.\nL1286: \nL1287: anything else\nL1288: \nL1289: \nL1290: cite308†Consume a token from `input` and return the result.\nL1291: #### 5.5.9. Consume a simple block\nL1292: \nL1293: To consume a simple block from a cite237†token stream `input`:\nL1294: \nL1295: Assert: the cite265†next token of `input` is cite131†<{-token> , cite203†<[-token> , or cite195†<(-token> .\nL1296: \nL1297: Let `ending token` be the mirror variant of the cite265†next token . (E.g. if it was called with cite203†<[-token> , the `ending token` is cite208†<]-token> .)\nL1298: Let `block` be a new cite249†simple block with its associated token set to the cite265†next token and with its value initially set to an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1299: \nL1300: cite268†Discard a token from `input`.\nL1301: \nL1302: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1303: \nL1304: cite153†<eof-token> L1305: `ending token`\nL1306: \nL1307: \nL1308: cite268†Discard a token from `input`. Return `block`.\nL1309: \nL1310: anything else\nL1311: \nL1312: \nL1313: cite304†Consume a component value from `input` and append the result to `block`’s value.\nL1314: #### 5.5.10. Consume a function\nL1315: \nL1316: To consume a function from a cite237†token stream `input`:\nL1317: \nL1318: Assert: The cite265†next token is a cite155†<function-token> .\nL1319: \nL1320: cite308†Consume a token from `input`, and let `function` be a new function with its name equal the returned token’s value, and a value set to an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1321: \nL1322: cite269†Process `input`:\nL1323: \nL1324: cite153†<eof-token> L1325: cite196†<)-token> L1326: \nL1327: \nL1328: cite268†Discard a token from `input`. Return `function`.\nL1329: \nL1330: anything else\nL1331: cite304†Consume a component value from `input` and append the result to `function`’s value.\nL1332: #### 5.5.11. Consume a cite236†unicode-range value\nL1333: \nL1334: To consume the value of a unicode-range descriptor from a string `input string`:\nL1335: \nL1336:   1. Let `tokens` be the result of cite270†tokenizing `input string` with `unicode ranges allowed` set to true.\nL1337: \nL1338:   2. cite305†Consume a list of component values from `tokens`, and return the result.\nL1339: \nL1340: Note: The existence of this algorithm is due to a design mistake in early CSS. It should never be reproduced.\nL1341: ## 6. The `An+B` microsyntax\nL1342: \nL1343: Several things in CSS, such as the cite339†:nth-child() pseudoclass, need to indicate indexes in a list. The `An+B` microsyntax is useful for this, allowing an author to easily indicate single elements or all elements at regularly-spaced intervals in a list.\nL1344: \nL1345: The An+B notation defines an integer step (`A`) and offset (`B`), and represents the `An+B`th elements in a list, for every positive integer or zero value of `n`, with the first element in the list having index 1 (not 0).\nL1346: For values of `A` and `B` greater than 0, this effectively divides the list into groups of `A` elements (the last group taking the remainder), and selecting the `B`th element of each group.\nL1347: \nL1348: The `An+B` notation also accepts the even and odd keywords, which have the same meaning as 2n and 2n+1, respectively.\nL1349: \nL1350: Examples:\nL1351: \nL1352:     2n+0   /* represents all of the even elements in the list */\nL1353:     even   /* same */\nL1354:     4n+1   /* represents the 1st, 5th, 9th, 13th, etc. elements in the list */\nL1355: The values of `A` and `B` can be negative, but only the positive results of `An+B`, for `n` ≥ 0, are used.\nL1356: \nL1357: Example:\nL1358: \nL1359:     -1n+6   /* represents the first 6 elements of the list */\nL1360:     -4n+10  /* represents the 2nd, 6th, and 10th elements of the list */\nL1361: \nL1362: \nL1363: If both `A` and `B` are 0, the pseudo-class represents no element in the list.\nL1364: ### 6.1. Informal Syntax Description\nL1365: \nL1366: This section is non-normative.\nL1367: \nL1368: When `A` is 0, the `An` part may be omitted (unless the `B` part is already omitted). When `An` is not included and `B` is non-negative, the cite340†+ sign before `B` (when allowed) may also be omitted. In this case the syntax simplifies to just `B`.\nL1369: \nL1370: Examples:\nL1371: \nL1372:     0n+5   /* represents the 5th element in the list */\nL1373:     5      /* same */\nL1374: \nL1375: When `A` is 1 or -1, the `1` may be omitted from the rule.\nL1376: \nL1377: Examples:\nL1378: The following notations are therefore equivalent:\nL1379: \nL1380:     1n+0   /* represents all elements in the list */\nL1381:     n+0    /* same */\nL1382:     n      /* same */\nL1383: \nL1384: If `B` is 0, then every `A`th element is picked. In such a case, the `+B` (or `-B`) part may be omitted unless the `A` part is already omitted.\nL1385: \nL1386: Examples:\nL1387: \nL1388:     2n+0   /* represents every even element in the list */\nL1389:     2n     /* same */\nL1390: \nL1391: When B is negative, its minus sign replaces the cite340†+ sign.\nL1392: \nL1393: Valid example:\nL1394: \nL1395:     3n-6\nL1396: Invalid example:\nL1397: \nL1398:     3n + -6\nL1399: \nL1400: Whitespace is permitted on either side of the cite340†+ or - that separates the `An` and `B` parts when both are present.\nL1401: \nL1402: Valid Examples with white space:\nL1403: \nL1404:     3n + 1\nL1405:     +3n - 2\nL1406:     -n+ 6\nL1407:     +6\nL1408: \nL1409: Invalid Examples with white space:\nL1410: \nL1411:     3 n\nL1412:     + 2n\nL1413:     + 2\nL1414: ### 6.2. The `<a-n-plus-b>` type\nL1415: \nL1416: The `An+B` notation was originally defined using a slightly different tokenizer than the rest of CSS, resulting in a somewhat odd definition when expressed in terms of CSS tokens. This section describes how to recognize the `An+B` notation in terms of CSS tokens (thus defining the `<a-n-plus-b>` type for CSS grammar purposes), and how to interpret the CSS tokens to obtain values for `A` and `B`.\nL1417: \nL1418: Tests\nL1419:   * cite341†anb-parsing.html†wpt.fyi cite342†(live test)†wpt.live cite343†(source)†github.com L1420:   * cite344†anb-serialization.html†wpt.fyi cite345†(live test)†wpt.live cite346†(source)†github.com L1421: The `<a-n-plus-b>` type is defined (using the cite347†Value Definition Syntax in the Values & Units spec†www.w3.org ) as:\nL1422: \nL1423:     <a-n-plus-b> =\nL1424:       odd cite348†| even cite348†| L1425:       `<integer>` cite348†| L1426: \nL1427:       `<n-dimension>` cite348†| L1428:       '+'cite349†? ^{cite350†‡ } n cite348†| L1429:       -n cite348†| L1430: \nL1431:       `<ndashdigit-dimension>` cite348†| L1432:       '+'cite349†? ^{cite350†‡ } `<ndashdigit-ident>` cite348†| L1433:       `<dashndashdigit-ident>` cite348†| L1434: \nL1435:       `<n-dimension>` `<signed-integer>` cite348†| L1436:       '+'cite349†? ^{cite350†‡ } n `<signed-integer>` cite348†| L1437:       -n `<signed-integer>` cite348†| L1438: \nL1439:       `<ndash-dimension>` `<signless-integer>` cite348†| L1440:       '+'cite349†? ^{cite350†‡ } n- `<signless-integer>` cite348†| L1441:       -n- `<signless-integer>` cite348†| L1442: \nL1443:       `<n-dimension>` ['+' cite348†| '-'] `<signless-integer>` cite348†| L1444:       '+'cite349†? ^{cite350†‡ } n ['+' cite348†| '-'] `<signless-integer>` cite348†| L1445:       -n ['+' cite348†| '-'] `<signless-integer>`\nL1446: where:\nL1447:   * `<n-dimension>` is a cite162†<dimension-token> with its type flag set to \"integer\", and a unit that is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"n\"\nL1448:   * `<ndash-dimension>` is a cite162†<dimension-token> with its type flag set to \"integer\", and a unit that is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"n-\"\nL1449:   * `<ndashdigit-dimension>` is a cite162†<dimension-token> with its type flag set to \"integer\", and a unit that is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"n-*\", where \"*\" is a series of one or more cite175†digits L1450:   * `<ndashdigit-ident>` is an cite154†<ident-token> whose value is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"n-*\", where \"*\" is a series of one or more cite175†digits L1451:   * `<dashndashdigit-ident>` is an cite154†<ident-token> whose value is an cite219†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for \"-n-*\", where \"*\" is a series of one or more cite175†digits L1452:   * `<integer>` is a cite160†<number-token> with its type flag set to \"integer\"\nL1453:   * `<signed-integer>` is a cite160†<number-token> with its type flag set to \"integer\", and a sign character\nL1454:   * `<signless-integer>` is a cite160†<number-token> with its type flag set to \"integer\", and no sign character\nL1455: ^{†}: When a plus sign (+) precedes an ident starting with \"n\", as in the cases marked above, there must be no whitespace between the two tokens, or else the tokens do not match the above grammar. Whitespace is valid (and ignored) between any other two tokens.\nL1456: \nL1457: The clauses of the production are interpreted as follows:\nL1458: odd\nL1459:     `A` is 2, `B` is 1.\nL1460: even\nL1461:     `A` is 2, `B` is 0.\nL1462: ``<integer>``\nL1463:     `A` is 0, `B` is the integer’s value.\nL1464: ``<n-dimension>``\nL1465: `'+'? n`\nL1466: `-n`\nL1467:     `A` is the dimension’s value, 1, or -1, respectively. `B` is 0.\nL1468: ``<ndashdigit-dimension>``\nL1469: `'+'? `<ndashdigit-ident>``\nL1470:     `A` is the dimension’s value or 1, respectively. `B` is the dimension’s unit or ident’s value, respectively, with the first cite104†code point†infra.spec.whatwg.org removed and the remainder interpreted as a base-10 number. B is negative.\nL1471: ``<dashndashdigit-ident>``\nL1472:     `A` is -1. `B` is the ident’s value, with the first two cite104†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org removed and the remainder interpreted as a base-10 number. B is negative.\nL1473: ``<n-dimension>` `<signed-integer>``\nL1474: `'+'? n `<signed-integer>``\nL1475: `-n `<signed-integer>``\nL1476:     `A` is the dimension’s value, 1, or -1, respectively. `B` is the integer’s value.\nL1477: ``<ndash-dimension>` `<signless-integer>``\nL1478: `'+'? n- `<signless-integer>``\nL1479: `-n- `<signless-integer>``\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Object Model (CSSOM) Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom/)\nciteturn1189view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1188view2\",\"id\":541}); Total lines: 2901\n"
